WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.975
A Class diagram describes types and the relationships
between them.

00:00:05.095 --> 00:00:11.445
It helps you explain a domain model or an interface
design, rather than the order of one request.

00:00:11.565 --> 00:00:16.965
Our example has Customer, Request, RequestStatus and a
repository interface.

00:00:17.085 --> 00:00:24.610
You will add an entity, inspect structured members, review
relationship choices, organize types and apply a shared
style.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:32.505
Keep the level of detail appropriate for the audience: not
every implementation field needs to appear in an
explanatory model.

00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:40.933
Use Insert and Class, interface, or enum to create
AuditRecord.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.278
Inspect the kind options before adding it.

00:00:44.398 --> 00:00:50.448
A class, abstract class, interface, enumeration and
annotation carry different meanings.

00:00:50.568 --> 00:00:55.843
Give the type a clear name and use an alias where it makes
source references easier.

00:00:55.963 --> 00:01:01.113
After creation, inspect the generated declaration and the
rendered object.

00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:08.708
Adding a type is only the beginning; its members and
relationships should explain the responsibility you intend
it to have.

00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:15.858
Select Customer to open the entity inspector.

00:01:15.978 --> 00:01:21.553
Identity includes the type kind, name, alias and generic
type information.

00:01:21.673 --> 00:01:29.748
The Members section presents fields and operations rather
than forcing you to treat the entire declaration as one
opaque label.

00:01:29.868 --> 00:01:35.693
Review visibility, static and abstract modifiers alongside
the actual member meaning.

00:01:35.813 --> 00:01:39.838
Presentation and package placement are separate choices.

00:01:39.958 --> 00:01:48.433
Make a small, intentional change and check the source
rather than editing several unrelated properties before
reviewing the result.

00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:57.558
A field describes stored or exposed state; a method
describes an operation.

00:01:57.678 --> 00:02:05.053
In the inspector, use the structured member controls to
distinguish these kinds and review their visibility and
type.

00:02:05.173 --> 00:02:11.498
We will add a description field and an assignment
operation to Request using the structured controls.

00:02:11.618 --> 00:02:14.543
Inspect the source and then the entity.

00:02:14.663 --> 00:02:24.088
Keep the declaration readable: a useful model tells the
reader what the type is responsible for, not just how many
members were copied from an implementation.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.925
The assignment operation takes an agent identifier and
returns no value.

00:02:33.045 --> 00:02:39.970
A parameter name and its type communicate different facts,
just as a method name differs from its return type.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:46.715
Use type completion and structured controls where
available, then inspect the resulting declaration.

00:02:46.835 --> 00:02:52.510
For generic types or specialized syntax, source remains
the precise editing path.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:59.505
A completion suggestion is a convenience, not evidence
that the chosen type is semantically right for the model.

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:07.933
Select Request and add a method named assignTo with return
type void.

00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:11.378
Use the new method row to add a parameter.

00:03:11.498 --> 00:03:20.998
Enter agentId as its name and UUID as its type, then click
outside the parameter group to commit and inspect the
resulting declaration in source.

00:03:21.118 --> 00:03:26.018
The parameter type and return type describe different
parts of the operation.

00:03:26.138 --> 00:03:31.513
Reveal the member in source and verify that its enclosing
class remains intact.

00:03:31.633 --> 00:03:38.108
Choose types that express your domain; a completion list
is an editing aid, not a design decision.

00:03:41.633 --> 00:03:46.708
The associations and dependency in this model express
different connections.

00:03:46.828 --> 00:03:54.328
Customer submits requests, Request uses a status, and the
repository depends on Request as a parameter type.

00:03:54.448 --> 00:04:01.173
Open the relationship tool to inspect available endpoint,
kind, label and multiplicity choices.

00:04:01.293 --> 00:04:08.843
Choose a relation because it describes the responsibility
or ownership you mean, not because its arrow happens to
look familiar.

00:04:08.963 --> 00:04:14.288
Reconnect mistaken endpoints and verify the source after
an identity change.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:22.050
Use a small proposal to check a relationship before
keeping it.

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:28.795
Choose the repository as the source, the status type as
the target and dependency as the relationship.

00:04:28.915 --> 00:04:33.940
Label it filters by, add it and inspect the authored arrow
and current preview.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:40.560
The label explains the proposed contract; decide whether
that contract fits the types in your model.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:47.480
Shared Undo removes this relationship as one authored
change, leaving the original domain model intact.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.500
A package or namespace groups related types.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:05:00.070
Notes explain an important assumption without turning a
class name into a paragraph.

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:06.040
The reference example places a note beside Request to
explain stable identity.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:10.860
Open package and note creation, then inspect Diagram
settings.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:20.630
Keep grouping consistent with the abstraction you are
teaching: a domain package, a code namespace and a
deployment boundary are not automatically the same thing.

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:25.900
Layout should support the relationship story without
inventing new semantics.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.725
Use modifier selection to choose Customer and Request
together.

00:05:33.845 --> 00:05:37.920
Inspect the bulk panel and apply a supported shared style.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:47.565
The style can distinguish domain types from infrastructure
interfaces, but labels and relationship meaning must
remain understandable without color alone.

00:05:47.685 --> 00:05:54.810
When copying or duplicating model elements, inspect the
identifiers, members and connections in the result.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:06:00.105
Use the source diff or Undo when the operation includes
more than you intended.

00:06:03.633 --> 00:06:11.658
Pause and add the audit record class, then give Request a
description field and an assignment operation with a typed
parameter.

00:06:11.778 --> 00:06:17.003
Explain the status enumeration and repository interface in
your own words.

00:06:17.123 --> 00:06:25.448
Review one relationship endpoints and multiplicity, add a
useful note, and apply a shared style to a selected pair
of entities.

00:06:25.568 --> 00:06:35.393
A successful result makes the type responsibilities and
relationships clear without requiring the reader to
reconstruct the implementation from a cluttered drawing.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:43.550
Use Class diagrams for types, members and structural
relationships.

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:49.170
Use Sequence or Activity diagrams when the important
question is behavioral flow.

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:58.240
The common editing pattern remains the same: create or
select an object, inspect supported properties, and check
the source and rendered result.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:07.810
The next lesson moves outward from types to components and
infrastructure, while retaining that habit of separating
identity, meaning and presentation.
